%I A005792 M0529 #47 Aug 18 2026 19:34:08
%S A005792 1,2,3,4,5,8,9,10,12,13,16,17,18,20,25,26,27,29,32,34,36,37,40,41,45,
%T A005792 48,49,50,52,53,58,61,64,65,68,72,73,74,75,80,81,82,85,89,90,97,98,
%U A005792 100,101,104,106,108,109,113,116,117,121,122,125,128,130,136,137,144,145
%N A005792 Positive numbers that are the sum of 2 squares or 3 times a square.
%C A005792 Equivalently, numbers of the form k^2, k^2+m^2, or 3*k^2, where k >= 1, m >= 1.
%C A005792 Theorem (Golomb; Snover et al.): A triangle can be partitioned into n triangles congruent to each other and similar to it iff n is of the form k^2, k^2+m^2, or 3*k^2. [corrected by _Vico Bonfioli_, Aug 12 2026]
%C A005792 From _Vico Bonfioli_, Aug 12 2026: (Start)
%C A005792 The similarity hypothesis is essential; without it more n occur, and this sequence is a proper subset of the counts realizable by congruent triangles. Not here but realizable: 28, 44, 77, 99.
%C A005792 The isosceles triangle (16,16,22) is cut into 44, and (24,24,33) into 99, triangles congruent to the (2,3,4) triangle; n = 28 and n = 77 are Beeson's triquadratic and four-component constructions.
%C A005792 (End)
%D A005792 N. J. A. Sloane and Simon Plouffe, The Encyclopedia of Integer Sequences, Academic Press, 1995 (includes this sequence).
%D A005792 A. Soifer, How Does One Cut A Triangle?, Chapter 2, CEME, Colorado Springs CO 1990.
%H A005792 Rémy Sigrist, <a href="/A005792/b005792.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A005792 Michael Beeson, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1206.2229">Triangle tiling III: the case 3*alpha + 2*beta = pi</a>, arXiv:1206.2229 [math.MG], 2019.
%H A005792 Vico Bonfioli, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20961918">A signed-direction invariant for triangle tilings, and the exclusion of primes congruent to 3 modulo 4</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A005792 Solomon W. Golomb, <a href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/3611700">Replicating figures in the plane</a>, The mathematical gazette 48.366 (1964): 403-412.
%H A005792 Murray Klamkin, <a href="/A074764/a074764.pdf">Review of "How Does One Cut a Triangle?" by Alexander Soifer</a>, Amer. Math. Monthly, October 1991, pp. 775-. [Annotated scanned copy of pages 775-777 only] See "Grand Problem 1".
%H A005792 S. Snover, <a href="/A005789/a005789.pdf">Letter to N. J. A. Sloane, May 1991</a>
%H A005792 S. L. Snover, C. Waiveris and J. K. Williams, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/0012-365X(91)90110-N">Rep-tiling for triangles</a>, Discrete Math. 91 (1991), no. 2, 193-200.
%H A005792 <a href="/index/Su#ssq">Index entries for sequences related to sums of squares</a>
%p A005792 filter:= proc(n) local F;
%p A005792   F:= ifactors(n)[2];
%p A005792   andmap(t -> (t[1] mod 4 <> 3 or t[2]::even), F)
%p A005792   or andmap(t -> t[1] = 3 or t[2]::even, F)
%p A005792 end proc:
%p A005792 select(filter, [$1..1000]); # _Robert Israel_, Aug 12 2026
%Y A005792 Union of positive terms of A000290, A000404, A033428.
